{"id": "boreal", "name": "Boreal Forest", "short": "Boreal Forest", "place": "Canada, with Alaska", "lat": 54.0, "lon": -100.0, "side": "r", "blurb": "Spruce, hares and lynx on a ten-year cycle, shaped by fire and frozen peat.", "tip": {"id": "hare", "title": "Crash the hares", "text": "Snowshoe hares are the main food of lynx. Set Snowshoe hare to -80% and watch the lynx, then see which other predators and prey feel it."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Snowshoe hare", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowshoe_hare"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Canada lynx", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada_lynx"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Taiga", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiga"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Canadian boreal forest", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canadian_boreal_forest"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Great grey owl", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_grey_owl"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Moose", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moose"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Boreal forest of Canada", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boreal_forest_of_Canada"}], "notes": "The story and why it matters come from the Wikipedia articles listed below (Boreal forest of Canada, Snowshoe hare, Canada lynx). Individual species and links have not been checked one by one. Feeding links that match a GloBI (Global Biotic Interactions) record are marked in the node details, with a link to the records. A few links were added from GloBI records.", "imgwiki": "Boreal forest of Canada", "icon": "moose", "tier": "overview", "cont": "North America", "country": "Canada", "nodes": [{"id": "blackspruce", "sp": "picea mariana", "xh": 0.08}, {"id": "jackpine", "sp": "pinus banksiana", "xh": 0.22}, {"id": "aspen", "sp": "populus tremuloides", "xh": 0.36, "name": "Trembling aspen", "wiki": "Quaking aspen"}, {"id": "tamarack", "sp": "larix laricina", "xh": 0.5, "ic": "willow"}, {"id": "lichmoss", "sp": "cladonia, sphagnum and others", "xh": 0.66}, {"id": "blueberry", "sp": "vaccinium spp.", "xh": 0.84}, {"id": "budworm", "sp": "choristoneura fumiferana", "xh": 0.06}, {"id": "vole", "sp": "myodes gapperi", "xh": 0.19, "name": "Red-backed vole", "wiki": "Southern red-backed vole", "ic": "vole"}, {"id": "hare", "sp": "lepus americanus", "xh": 0.32, "wiki": "Snowshoe hare", "desc": "Eats grasses and herbs in summer and buds, twigs and bark in winter. Its numbers rise and fall in a cycle of roughly ten years, and lynx numbers follow.", "ic": "vole", "badge": "Cyclic keystone prey"}, {"id": "rsquirrel", "sp": "tamiasciurus hudsonicus", "xh": 0.45, "name": "Red squirrel", "wiki": "American red squirrel", "desc": "Harvests conifer cones and seeds and caches them for winter. Prey for lynx and owls.", "ic": "rsquirrel"}, {"id": "beaver", "sp": "castor canadensis", "xh": 0.58, "name": "North American beaver", "wiki": "North American beaver", "desc": "Fells aspen and other trees and dams streams, creating ponds that change the surrounding forest.", "badge": "Ecosystem engineer"}, {"id": "moose", "sp": "alces alces", "xh": 0.72, "desc": "A browser of willow, birch and aspen shoots and water plants. Wolves and bears take calves, and young forest after fire gives it more food."}, {"id": "caribou", "sp": "rangifer tarandus caribou", "xh": 0.87}, {"id": "warbler", "sp": "setophaga and leiothlypis spp.", "xh": 0.14}, {"id": "owl", "sp": "strix nebulosa", "xh": 0.38}, {"id": "bear", "sp": "ursus americanus", "xh": 0.66, "name": "American black bear", "tl": 3, "wiki": "American black bear", "desc": "An omnivore that eats berries and plants in summer. It also takes moose calves in spring and scavenges kills."}, {"id": "lynx", "sp": "lynx canadensis", "xh": 0.32, "tl": 4, "badge": "Specialist predator"}, {"id": "wolf", "sp": "canis lupus", "xh": 0.72}, {"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.08, "desc": "The short summer growing season sets how much the forest can grow."}, {"id": "fire", "sp": "wildfire", "xh": 0.32}, {"id": "peat", "sp": "permafrost & peatla